{
 "source": "he",
 "accent": "he-IL",
 "status": "ready",
 "version": 1,
 "derives_from": "The Academy of the Hebrew Language's own rules for transcribing from foreign languages into Hebrew -- כללי התעתיק מלועזית לעברית, redebated in the plenum's 5764-5767 sessions, approved 4 Sivan 5767 (21 May 2007) and amended in session 355 on 30 October 2017. **It is already the right shape, which is rare here**: the principle the rules are built on is that a name is transcribed אין מתעתקים את השמות על פי כתיבם במקורם כי אם על פי הגייתם הנשמעת לנו -- not by its spelling in the source but by how it sounds to us -- which is this column's job in the Academy's own words. So the letters here are its letters: `ג׳` /dʒ/, `צ׳` /tʃ/, `ז׳` /ʒ/, `ת׳` /θ/, `ד׳` /ð/, `נג` /ŋ/, `ו` for /v/ and /w/ alike, `ט` for /t/ and `ק` for /k/ (the rules reserve `ת` and `כּ` for etymology), `י` as the mater for /i/, `ו` for /o/ and /u/, and `א` as the seat for a syllable that opens with a vowel -- הברה הנפתחת בתנועה תיכתב בא׳.",
 "deviations": [
  {
   "rule": "The rules leave the niqqud optional and are written for names in running text, which is unpointed",
   "why": "**Pointed, always.** Unpointed Hebrew does not write /a/ or /e/ at all and writes /i/ and /e/ with the same `י` and /o/ and /u/ with the same `ו`, so the rules' own output cannot tell `ken` from `kin` -- which is fine for a name a reader already knows and useless for a pronunciation. The Academy permits the pointing as an option and this table takes it, on the same argument the Arabic table makes for its harakat: full pointing is the marked, pedagogical register in Hebrew as full vocalisation is in Arabic, so a pointed string reads unmistakably as a pronunciation guide rather than as a spelling. The letters stay the rules' full spelling -- `ו` for /o/, `י` for /i/ -- so this is ktiv male menuqad, which is also what the `he` pack's own `text_alt` column is."
  },
  {
   "rule": "The rules write a foreign /x/ as `ח׳`, to keep it apart from Hebrew's own `ח`",
   "why": "**Plain `ח`**, which the rules give as the permitted alternative. Hebrew's `ח` *is* [χ] in Modern Israeli Hebrew, so the plain letter is what makes a reader say the sound; the geresh exists to protect the etymology of a name, which a disposable hint has none of. The three geresh letters that are everyday Hebrew -- `ג׳ צ׳ ז׳` -- are kept, and so are `ת׳` and `ד׳`, which are the only notation Hebrew has for /θ/ and /ð/."
  },
  {
   "rule": "No stress device",
   "why": "**None, and it is not for want of looking.** The script is caseless, so `caps` is out. `acute` and `grave` are byte-identical no-ops: `VOWEL_LETTERS` in core/respell.js lists the letters a diacritic may land on and Hebrew's vowels are not letters at all -- they are marks on the consonant, and the slots above and below it are already taken. `prime` is available and is rejected on collision: U+02B9 is indistinguishable from the geresh at 5pt, and this table uses the geresh on five letters to mean *this is a foreign consonant*, so a prime would read as one. The meteg U+05BD is Hebrew's own dictionary mark for stress and was the closest call; it needs a new device in core/respell.js for one reader, and a general Israeli reader does not decode it. Arabic reached the same answer for the same kind of reason."
  },
  {
   "rule": "CONTRIBUTING.md's separator table gives Arabic no hyphen, because it breaks the cursive join",
   "why": "**Hebrew takes the hyphen.** It is the other right-to-left script here and the reasoning does not carry: Hebrew is not cursive, has no joining forms and loses nothing to a break. Israeli teaching material divides syllables with exactly this hyphen, and `core/measure.js` treats it as a line-break opportunity, which a column setting a four-syllable Russian word at 5pt needs. The maqaf U+05BE was the alternative and is rejected twice over: it is Hebrew's mark for *joining* two words rather than dividing one, and it sits at letter-top height, where at 5pt it reads as another mark on the letter before it."
  }
 ],
 "approximations": [
  {
   "sound": "/θ/ /ð/",
   "spelt": "ת׳ ד׳",
   "why": "The Academy's own letters. A reader who has not met them says [t] and [d], which is the nearest Hebrew has."
  },
  {
   "sound": "/w/",
   "spelt": "ו",
   "why": "Hebrew has no /w/ and `ו` is /v/. The Academy writes /w/ with it regardless -- וושינגטון -- so a reader says [v], as Israelis do in every borrowed word."
  },
  {
   "sound": "the twelve vowel qualities Hebrew has no letter for",
   "why": "Five vowels take fifty. /æ ɑ ʌ ɐ/ all become patach, /ɛ ə œ ø/ segol, /ɪ y ɨ ɯ/ hiriq, /ɔ ɤ/ holam and /ʊ ʉ/ shuruk -- so München is מִינְכֶן and Köln is קֶלְן, which is what Hebrew print writes for both. Length is not written at all, because Modern Hebrew has none."
  },
  {
   "sound": "/ɲ/ /ʎ/ /c/ /ɟ/",
   "why": "Written consonant plus `י`, which is how Hebrew renders a palatal it lacks: `ניו` for Spanish ñ. The reader says [nj], [lj], [tj], [dj]."
  },
  {
   "sound": "Russian palatalisation, aspiration, ejectives, the retroflexes",
   "why": "Nothing is written. Hebrew contrasts none of them and the script has no mark for any of them; the plain consonant is what a reader can say."
  }
 ],
 "legend": "הכתיב כאן מנוקד בכוונה: הניקוד הוא ההגייה. ג׳ = j, צ׳ = ch, ז׳ = zh, ת׳ = th, ד׳ = the th של that, נג = ng. המקף מפריד הברות.",
 "note": "תעתיק הגייה בלבד, לא כתיב.",
 "policy": {
  "syllable_separator": "-",
  "word_separator": " ",
  "stress": "none",
  "stress_min_syllables": 2,
  "length": "none",
  "tone": "drop",
  "max_onset": 2,
  "fixups": "all",
  "locale": "he"
 },
